\chapter{Functional Modules}
% ============================================================

\section{Authentication Module}
The authentication module supports registration and login. JWT-based authentication is used so that authenticated users can access protected financial data.

\section{Transaction Module}
Users can add, view, update and delete transactions. Transactions can be categorized so that spending can later be analyzed by category and time period.

\section{Budget Module}
Users can define budgets for relevant spending categories and monitor whether their actual spending remains within the selected limits.

\section{Goal Module}
Users can create financial goals and track progress towards them. Goal information is also used by the analysis and scenario modules.

\section{Analytics Module}
The analytics layer calculates financial metrics such as monthly spending, category-wise spending, average spending, savings and spending trends. It also identifies recurring patterns.

\section{Anomaly Detection}
The system checks transaction and spending behaviour for unusual changes. For example, an increase in spending beyond a defined threshold can be treated as a possible anomaly.

\section{Rule Engine}
The rule engine converts financial conditions into explainable actions. Example rules include:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
    \item If Food spending $>$ Food budget, generate a warning.
    \item If spending increases by more than 20\%, detect an anomaly.
    \item If goal probability $<$ 70\%, suggest increasing savings.
\end{itemize}

Users can view, edit, enable or disable rules.

\section{Scenario Simulation}
The system can compare possible financial scenarios. Monte Carlo simulation can produce probability ranges such as P10, P50 and P90 projections for future outcomes.

\section{Recommendation Module}
Recommendations are generated from financial metrics, rules, anomalies and scenario results. The system is intended to make recommendations explainable by showing the relevant rule or financial condition.

